<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.11.1: Maximum and Minimum Value of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02518</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.11.1: Maximum and Minimum Value of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ff9412ca-5c12-4cdd-b742-38106fc6d023</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Examine the graph that represents the function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_13_02_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00003">Use this graph to answer questions 1 - 2.</para>
      <exercise id="_72cfface-ac05-4874-bfc9-69aefdf085cd" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> can be expressed in words as "the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 1." Find or compute:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 1</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Correct! 10</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Nice try! The correct answer is 10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_76f54561-f72a-466f-9fcd-fd6630ad1296" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00011">Correct! 2</para>
          <para id="para-00012">Nice try! The correct answer is 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e58fc7e0-0d49-42d8-9823-f5a38da3008a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00015">Correct! 37</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Nice try! The correct answer is 37.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_21d017b5-41d2-47d0-a0e4-43abc15a47f1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Can you find an <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value that would make <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Less than 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">Correct! No, from the graph, it seems that 1 is the least value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and that occurs when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. All other <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values produce outputs that are greater than 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00020">Incorrect. The correct answer is no. From the graph, it seems that 1 is the least value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and that occurs when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. All other <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values produce outputs that are greater than 1.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_1700560e-dce8-4bb6-90bb-f975755e10a5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Greater than 10,000?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Correct! Yes, there are lots of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values that produce an output greater than 10,000. For example, both -100 and 110 would make <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> greater than 10,000.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Incorrect. The correct answer is yes. There are lots of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values that produce an output greater than 10,000. For example, both -100 and 110 would make <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> greater than 10,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00024">Examine the graph that represents the function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00025">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_13_02_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00026">Use this graph to answer questions 3 and 4.</para>
      <exercise id="ae384efe-9cca-4fce-a616-2fb8bf7b91ad" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00027">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00028"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> can be expressed in words as "the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 9." Find or compute:</para>
          <para id="para-00029">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 9</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00030">Correct! -2</para>
          <para id="para-00031">Nice try! The correct answer is -2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_2f94da66-a9e7-44d8-88bf-37d694c46cd1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00032">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>13</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>13</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00034">Correct! 6</para>
          <para id="para-00035">Nice try! The correct answer is 6.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_7a56b374-7d86-4699-8298-850f71990e6d" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00036">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00038">Correct! -93</para>
          <para id="para-00039">Nice try! The correct answer is -93.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_61ccfbc5-6435-4b4e-84ee-0e63ff6c2186" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00040">Can you find an <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-value that would make <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>:</para>
          <para id="para-00041">Greater than 7?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00042">Correct! No, it seems that 7 is the greatest value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and that occurs when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. All other <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values produce outputs that are less than 7.</para>
          <para id="para-00043">Incorrect. The correct answer is no. From the graph, it seems that 7 is the greatest value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and that occurs when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. All other <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values produce outputs that are less than 7.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_1074845e-8676-4e41-80d7-b588891b1e28" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00044">Less than -10,000?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00045">Correct! Yes, there are lots of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values that produce an output less than -10,000. For example, both -200 and 200 would make <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> greater than -10,000.</para>
          <para id="para-00046">Incorrect. The correct answer is yes. There are lots of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values that produce an output less than -10,000. For example, both -200 and 200 would make <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> greater than -10,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>